WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.000
- Well hello there.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.290
My inbox just got bombarded because

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:06.170
Conan O'Brien vlogged his doctors visit.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:07.270
So I thought I'd watch it

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.120
and react to it.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.890
Perhaps even a good learning opportunity.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.162
Let's get started.

00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:12.412
Elevator Music)

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.940
- Anyone notice yet today that my voice

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.161
sounds a little different?

00:00:20.161 --> 00:00:21.020
Does it sound a little different to you?

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:21.853
A little sexier?

00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:22.686
A little lower?

00:00:22.686 --> 00:00:23.697
Thanks a lot.

00:00:23.697 --> 00:00:24.970
(Laughter)

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:25.803
There's a reason for that.

00:00:25.803 --> 00:00:28.098
I hadn't actually felt well today.

00:00:28.098 --> 00:00:29.608
(Awww)

00:00:29.608 --> 00:00:30.441
(Wah Wah)

00:00:30.441 --> 00:00:31.274
- You bastards.

00:00:31.274 --> 00:00:32.898
Yeah, womp womp.

00:00:32.898 --> 00:00:34.280
(Laughter)

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.330
We're talking about my physical health.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.270
- And little does Conan know,

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.160
his physical health is intimately tied in

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.380
with his mental health.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.270
So if he's not performing well physically,

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.700
it can make him quite upset,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.850
which can lead to feelings of worry,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:48.683
anxiety,

00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:50.310
even depression in some cases.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.723
- I just have some kind
of virus or something.

00:00:54.264 --> 00:00:55.860
And last night I was up all night.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.960
Really sore throat.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.770
I woke up today achy.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.130
Just don't feel well.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.980
Come to work cause the show must go on.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.450
- What is sounds like that he's describing

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.830
could be either a viral illness

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.580
or it could be bacterial illness.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.400
The fact that he's saying body aches

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.480
and sore throat.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.110
He's putting all of
these systems together.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.890
Sounds like I'm leaning towards viral.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.220
But it's hard to know from
his limited description here.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.080
The one thing I will tell
you that some of the most

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.730
painful sore throats happen as a result

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.350
of a viral illness,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.720
not strep throat.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.530
So don't jump to concluding that

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.500
because your throat is really sore,

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.290
it must mean you have strep throat.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.340
Actually it's not that case.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.370
It's actually the other way.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.796
- A lot of Nyquil.

00:01:35.796 --> 00:01:36.629
(Laughter)

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:39.260
I woke up with my pants around my ankles.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.640
- Did you feel better?

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:41.810
- I felt better.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.310
So did the guy I was with.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.143
- Oh!

00:01:44.143 --> 00:01:45.860
He just threw in one of those didn't he?

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.890
Please don't take a lot of Nyquil folks.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.480
Cause Nyquil is actually
a combination medicine.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.560
It has what is the
equivalent of Benadryl in it

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.530
and Tylenol.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.660
So it not only is a pain reliever,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.040
but it also puts you to sleep.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.190
However Benadryl,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:00.440
or Nyquil for that matter,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.350
if you take it and you don't go to sleep

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.630
and you stay up,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.400
it's actually worse for you in terms

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.820
of concentration and
ability to perform tasks

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:08.653
than alcohol.

00:02:08.653 --> 00:02:10.110
There's actually been a study done

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.300
that tested people who
were under the influence

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.880
of alcohol in comparison to those

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.910
who took benadryl,

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.270
And those who took
benadryl were worse drivers

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.590
than the alcohol group.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:19.450
Isn't that crazy?

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.643
- Do you feel comfortable talking

00:02:20.643 --> 00:02:22.850
about the stomach stuff that was going on?

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:23.970
Like was it nausea,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.032
diarrhea?

00:02:25.032 --> 00:02:26.890
What was that symptom?

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.310
- You really want me
to talk about diarrhea

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:29.260
on television?

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.170
- When we're seeing the symptoms

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.810
of an upper respiratory infection

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.540
start spreading to the GI tract,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.440
what that points my mind
to is to a specific virus

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.880
called Adenovirus.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.710
Now this is one that affects both

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.600
upper respiratory and GI tract

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.010
and can cause diarrhea.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.700
In fact diarrhea and upper respiratory

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.090
symptoms point you to the direction

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.580
of thinking that this
could be caused by a virus.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.300
Because if you have just an infection

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.260
of bacteria in your throat,

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.580
it's very unlikely that it's gonna start

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.650
spreading into the GI system right away.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:58.940
So the fact that he's having multiple

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.580
system involvement here really makes me

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.450
lean towards the idea
that this is a virus.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.330
- Do you ever find yourself
talking to patients

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.570
and they mention that they've had diarrhea

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.175
and you start laughing?

00:03:08.175 --> 00:03:09.252
- Not anymore.

00:03:09.252 --> 00:03:10.835
- Oh.

00:03:10.835 --> 00:03:13.160
That's sad when all the
joy goes out of your life.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.000
- I don't think I've ever laughed

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.430
when a patient tells me diarrhea

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.130
unless they describe it in a funny way

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.210
where they're trying to be funny.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.470
Then we can share a laugh.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.460
I try and do some kind of humor

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:22.293
with it.

00:03:22.293 --> 00:03:23.640
Like are you just constantly running?

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.120
Are you basically having
diarrhea in your pants?

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.690
The patients and I both get a good laugh

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.810
and they understand that
this is very disarming.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.980
Functions as a way to
break down this barrier

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.970
that often exists between
patients and doctors

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.320
where we have to be very strict

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.580
and very proper.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.510
We can have a good time.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.343
We can laugh.

00:03:40.343 --> 00:03:42.030
- If this somehow could
all end with you saying

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.650
well you're an amazing male specimen,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:44.730
and I had that on camera,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:45.810
that would be great.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.890
But that's you decide.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.360
- An amazing male specimen.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.870
Can you imagine if like a doctor does

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.580
a prostate exam and then
afterwards he's like

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.840
you're an amazing male specimen.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.680
I think that would be really
weird feedback to get.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.550
We're trained not to use
weird language like that

00:03:58.550 --> 00:03:59.930
during our practicals.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:00.950
Like for example,

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.900
if I'm listening to someone's heart,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.750
I'm looking inside their ears,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.500
I'm doing a vaginal exam.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.290
I'm always trained to say
everything looks normal,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.640
or everything sounds normal.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.770
Instead of saying good.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.330
When you say everything looks normal,

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.460
that's less provocative,

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:17.320
there's no real wrong way to take normal.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.170
- Let's start with
taking your temperature.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.980
- Why'd they have the
Scrubs poster right there

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.380
that's even hanging crooked for effect?

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:25.423
That's awesome.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.153
(Laughter)

00:04:27.153 --> 00:04:27.986
He looks so miserable.

00:04:32.482 --> 00:04:33.315
(Laughter)

00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:34.148
(Beeping)

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:35.180
- [Doctor] Right on.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.510
97.5 you're actually cold.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.580
- A lot of wax in there.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.100
- I wonder if he's wincing
cause she's putting

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:40.933
it in deep,

00:04:40.933 --> 00:04:42.250
or he's just trying to be funny with it.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.940
Cause generally that procedure shouldn't

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:44.940
be that uncomfortable.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.040
You can get a good view
of the tympanic membrane

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.240
or the eardrum without pushing too far

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.300
and causing discomfort.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.430
It's actually one of the mistakes

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:52.900
that a lot of my medical students make

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.350
that I have to tell them why are you

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.330
pushing in so deep?

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.760
Think about how uncomfortable that feels

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:57.593
to the patient.

00:04:57.593 --> 00:04:59.490
And when they realize that sweet spot,

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.270
then you can do it
consistently over and over

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.570
again without causing
discomfort to a patient.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:05.809
- Stick your tongue out.

00:05:05.809 --> 00:05:06.642
Ready?

00:05:06.642 --> 00:05:08.510
- [Conan O'Brien] (gagging)

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.310
- The best thing you can do when a doctor

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.320
is trying to look into
the back of your throat,

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.970
keep your tongue out the whole time

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.477
and consistently say the sound ah.

00:05:16.477 --> 00:05:17.310
(Ah)

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.010
The reason why we say say ah,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.410
is cause it lifts your pallet.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:22.480
And when it lifts your pallet,

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.790
we have a really good view of the back

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:25.623
of your throat.

00:05:25.623 --> 00:05:27.120
I'll say to my patients open up,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:27.953
they'll open their mouth,

00:05:27.953 --> 00:05:28.786
they'll stick their tongue out,

00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:29.770
and I'll say say ah,

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.101
and they'll go ah.

00:05:31.101 --> 00:05:33.280
I didn't get any view of anything.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:34.870
- What're you feeling there?

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.340
- Feeling for swollen lymph nodes,

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.520
glands.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:38.353
Anything tender?

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.273
- Not yet.

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:41.550
- If they were swollen and enlarged,

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.900
that would be indicative
of a form of infection

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:44.870
or inflammation.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.110
Glands do get swollen during that time,

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:48.860
but they should return back to normal

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.120
after a period of time.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.020
If your glands don't return to normal

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:52.990
and they stay swollen,

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:54.063
that's a reason to go see your doctor.

00:05:54.063 --> 00:05:56.660
- There's a tattoo there.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.989
It says REO Speedwagon rules.

00:05:58.989 --> 00:05:59.822
(Laughter)

00:05:59.822 --> 00:06:01.488
I got it in 1978.

00:06:01.488 --> 00:06:02.986
I thought that band was
gonna go the distance.

00:06:02.986 --> 00:06:04.022
- [Doctor] Oh my God.

00:06:04.022 --> 00:06:04.855
Two more.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.020
- One of the most important things

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.050
that you wanna tell patients

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.550
when you're listening to their lungs

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:11.770
using a stethoscope,

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.290
is to make sure they take deep breaths

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.220
through their mouth.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.920
This is the largest airway.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.750
There's no mucus in there unlike the nose

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:18.700
which is a smaller airway,

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.750
there's mucus in there,

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.440
and you can get upper airway sounds

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:22.980
like (sniffing).

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.700
That sound gets
transmitted into your lungs

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:26.533
and you'll hear it,

00:06:26.533 --> 00:06:27.840
and you won't be able to decipher

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.150
is that coming from the nose

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:29.983
or from the lungs?

00:06:29.983 --> 00:06:31.450
- I want as much medication as I could

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.705
possibly have.

00:06:32.705 --> 00:06:33.538
- Really?

00:06:33.538 --> 00:06:34.960
- A lot of my patients come in asking

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:35.900
for medication.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.550
Its a common misconception
that if you have

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.100
a virus or if you have a strep throat,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.700
that unless you get antibiotics,

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:43.920
you're not gonna get better.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:44.960
This is not the case.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:45.950
If you're otherwise healthy,

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.220
you're always gonna get better.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:48.810
In fact if you have strep throat,

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.920
for which we give antibiotics for,

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:53.330
we are only shorting the
course of strep throat

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.970
illness by 16 hours.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.010
It normally lasts 7 to 8 days.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.670
And we're saving you 16 hours of symptoms

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.930
by giving you the antibiotic.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:03.410
The antibiotic is not for your symptoms,

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:04.430
ladies and gentlemen.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:06.770
The antibiotic is there
strictly to prevent

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.178
the complications of strep throat.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:10.011
- You know,

00:07:10.011 --> 00:07:10.844
sometimes you hear about
like there's a cool

00:07:10.844 --> 00:07:13.600
Tylenol that they only sell in Sweden,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.290
that has weed in it,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:16.367
a little bit of cocaine.

00:07:16.367 --> 00:07:18.784
- (Laughter)

00:07:20.026 --> 00:07:21.276
- I don't think it'll help you.

00:07:21.276 --> 00:07:22.109
- No?

00:07:22.109 --> 00:07:22.942
- No.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:23.775
- Can I have it anyway?

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:24.608
(Laughter)

00:07:24.608 --> 00:07:26.340
- I've had patients say
that they heard about

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.450
some cool supplement that promises

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:30.400
to shorten the duration of a cold

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.130
if they take mega doses of it.

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:32.963
No.

00:07:32.963 --> 00:07:33.920
There's no magic fix.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.160
There's no magic vitamin.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.700
Its about getting the proper rest,

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:37.720
hydration,

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.610
getting the right amount of calories in,

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.460
and allowing your body to do its job.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.232
(Laughter)

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:47.065
- Nervous?

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:47.898
- A little bit.

00:07:47.898 --> 00:07:49.350
You haven't said anything for a while.

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:51.350
- The reason why we don't
say anything for a while

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.223
is cause there's a few
areas we have to listen to.

00:07:53.223 --> 00:07:55.500
We have to listen to on the right side

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:56.333
of your chest.

00:07:56.333 --> 00:07:57.410
Then we have to move over to the left side

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:58.243
of your chest.

00:07:58.243 --> 00:07:59.320
And there's 4 places that we listen to

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:00.820
on the left side of the chest.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:02.350
So unless we do all this,

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:04.240
we have to take a few
seconds to actually hear

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:05.073
the rhythm,

00:08:05.073 --> 00:08:06.270
make sure we don't hear a murmur,

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.800
cause that's what we're listening to,

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.390
and to make sure the rate is normal,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:12.360
not only in terms of
how fast it's beating,

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:13.570
but the rhythm as well.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:14.927
A heartbeat should be boom boom,

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:16.120
boom boom,

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:16.953
boom boom,

00:08:16.953 --> 00:08:17.970
it should keep a proper rhythm.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.056
If all of a sudden you're getting

00:08:19.056 --> 00:08:22.080
(sporadic boom)

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.060
that could be a sign
of atrial fibrillation

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.350
which is it's own cardiac arrhythmia

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.020
and all sorts of other issues

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.220
in which case we would then wanna get

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.840
extra testing like an EKG,

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.170
perhaps an echocardiogram,

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.930
depending on what we're suspecting.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:36.380
But the biggest reason we listen to

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.150
is for murmurs.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.910
When you have something
wrong with the valves

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:40.743
of your heart,

00:08:40.743 --> 00:08:42.280
or actually the chambers of your heart,

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.280
we hear turbulent flow.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.780
Meaning that if you ever hear fluid move

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.970
through a pipe and the
pipe is nice and clear,

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:49.910
you're not gonna hear anything.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:50.743
It's silent.

00:08:50.743 --> 00:08:51.640
But if something all of a sudden gets

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.670
in the way and the water
has to move around it,

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.290
that turbulence creates a sound.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:57.100
That's what we're
listening to in your chest.

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:58.980
- Do you have Mitral Valve
Prolapse or anything?

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.482
Has anyone told you that?

00:09:00.482 --> 00:09:01.315
- What's a prolapse?

00:09:01.315 --> 00:09:02.891
- It's one of the valves in the heart

00:09:02.891 --> 00:09:03.850
is just a little bit floppy.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.250
- I just came here to shoot,

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.990
I came here to shoot like a silly little

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:09.560
I've gotta cold and we're gonna just

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:10.521
screw around.

00:09:10.521 --> 00:09:11.585
You're telling me something real?

00:09:11.585 --> 00:09:12.931
- Yeah but it's nothing.

00:09:12.931 --> 00:09:13.764
You don't have a murmur or anything.

00:09:13.764 --> 00:09:15.880
- So Mitral Valve Prolapse,

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:16.960
what she's talking about here

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.790
is technically floppy heart valves.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.350
But we're specifically talking about

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:22.530
the left side of the heart

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:23.820
in between the lower portion,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:24.950
which is the left ventricle,

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:26.330
and the left atrium.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.370
There is something known
as the Mitral Valve.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:29.750
And it's two little leaflets.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:31.450
And sometimes they do get floppy

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.170
and they don't sit evenly with each other

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.760
and they actually prolapse
to the left atrium.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.400
And when that happens
is you can have a little

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.110
bit of leakage of blood that goes

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.540
into the left atrium
when it's not suppose to.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.510
Most of the time,

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:43.600
patients are asymptomatic,

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:45.010
meaning they don't even know they have it.

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:46.310
And we just find it kind of on a

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.060
routine physical exam and
no treatment is needed.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.907
That's why she wasn't so worried about it.

00:09:50.907 --> 00:09:52.262
[Conan O'Brien] I have a floppy heart.

00:09:52.262 --> 00:09:53.095
(Laughter)

00:09:53.095 --> 00:09:54.327
I have a lot of tension with my job.

00:09:54.327 --> 00:09:56.253
I have a lot of inner rage.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.030
I'm Irish Catholic stock.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.690
I'm worried we don't do
well in the long run.

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:03.970
We're not people that live a long time.

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:05.680
- I think it's really important to talk

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.750
about someone's mental state.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:09.300
Because if they're not in
a healthy mental state,

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.500
they're gonna take longer to recover

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:11.540
from their symptoms.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.400
They're gonna have worse
symptoms on average.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:14.790
So I like to get that in the open

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.300
and see if there's anything we can offer

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:17.133
this patient.

00:10:17.133 --> 00:10:18.780
[Conan O'Brien] A little fatigued.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:20.152
- Those are bad pushups.

00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:22.080
(Laughter)

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:22.913
- What do you think?

00:10:22.913 --> 00:10:24.330
I'm a pretty good male.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:25.230
- Yeah.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:26.690
Really fantastic.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:27.560
I think maybe,

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:28.580
- Look at the camera, please.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:29.520
- Male specimen.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.195
- You think maybe I'm a male specimen?

00:10:31.195 --> 00:10:33.376
(Laughter)

00:10:33.376 --> 00:10:35.500
[Doctor] This is the
sort of wimpy Sudafed,

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:36.970
cause we can't really get the real Sudafed

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.250
anymore because of the
crystal meth problem.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.060
- So I can't make
crystal meth out of this?

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.811
- Conan O'Brien is a
fantastic male specimen.

00:10:43.811 --> 00:10:45.459
- He got her to say it!

00:10:45.459 --> 00:10:47.280
(Laughter)

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.910
- Conan was awesome for showing this.

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.210
Showing his medical visit,

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:51.570
joking about it,

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.470
talking about his symptoms.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:55.120
This is how we make changes

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:55.960
in the medical community.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.060
By opening it up.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.930
By being more transparent.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.190
By showing doctors have a sense of humor.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.570
I have two videos that you have to watch.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:04.600
First is right here,

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.260
Untold Stories of the ER.

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:07.750
This one is fun.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:08.960
And then the one that is really unique

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.050
is this woman who can
smell Parkinson's Disease.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:13.810
Yeah she can actually smell it.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.030
Click on one of these bad boys now.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.677
And stay happy and healthy.

00:11:17.677 --> 00:11:20.427
(elevator music)

